issues.problematic_code_snippet:
abstract class TauriActivity : WryActivity() {
  var pluginManager: PluginManager = PluginManager(this)
  override val handleBackNavigation: Boolean = true

findings.body:
In `setup`, `handle_ref` is `&handle`, and `*handle_ref` tries to move `PluginHandle<R>` out of a shared borrow. `PluginHandle` is not `Copy` (plugin.rs:131 only gives it a manual `Clone`), so this is error E0507 and the Android target fails to compile. Moving out of `*handle_ref` is not allowed even with `Clone` in place. The `handle_ref` indirection serves no purpose either.

::diff{file="crates/tauri/src/app/plugin.rs" lines="135-143"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The `_app`/`_api` names are also misleading because both are used inside the cfg block. Rename them to `app` and `api`, and keep the underscore prefix only where the value is unused on that target.

`onBackButtonPress` delegates to `addPluginListener('app', 'back-button', ...)`, which invokes `plugin:app|register_listener`. The only implementation is the Kotlin `Plugin.registerListener`. Nothing in `crates/tauri/src` implements `register_listener` for the `app` plugin on non-Android targets. Calling the API on desktop therefore produces a command-not-found rejection.

The `try/catch` in `addPluginListener` does not help, because `invoke` returns a promise and the rejection happens asynchronously, outside the `try` block.

::diff{file="packages/api/src/app.ts" lines="267-277"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The JSDoc says "Listens to the backButton event on Android" but does not say the API is Android-only or that it will reject elsewhere. Callers writing cross-platform code will hit this at runtime.

When a `back-button` listener is registered, `handleOnBackPressed` takes the `hasListener` branch and never calls `goBack()` or the default activity action. The payload carries `canGoBack`, but the TS API exposes no method to perform navigation or close the app. An app that registers a listener and does nothing else can never be navigated back or exited with the hardware button. This is the same defect Capacitor avoids by providing a `goBack`/`exit` surface alongside the event.

::diff{file="crates/tauri/mobile/android/src/main/java/app/tauri/AppPlugin.kt" lines="30-43"}

::callout{type="info"}
The `exit` command in this file is the obvious candidate for this fallback, but it is not reachable from JS (see the finding on `exit`).

`setup` runs for every app that uses the core `app` plugin, so `AppPlugin`'s init registers an enabled `OnBackPressedCallback` in all Tauri Android apps. `TauriActivity` also sets `handleBackNavigation = true`. Apps that never use `onBackButtonPress` now have every back press routed through this callback. The fallback mostly matches the old behaviour, but it is a global change that is not gated by any opt-in. The interaction with wry's own back handling under `handleBackNavigation` is also not explained in the PR. Two back handlers could fire, or the wry bump could change which one wins.

::diff{file="crates/tauri/mobile/android-codegen/TauriActivity.kt" lines="12-16"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The wry bump (0.53.2 to 0.53.4) is not explained in the description. The `handleBackNavigation` property presumably depends on it. Verify that the two handlers do not both run.

`AppPlugin.exit` is annotated `@Command` but nothing exposes it. There is no TS binding in `packages/api/src/app.ts`, and `build.rs` and `reference.md` list no `exit` permission. Mobile plugin commands are gated by the ACL, so `plugin:app|exit` is denied by default. The changelog entry `android-app-plugin.md` says the plugin supports "exit and back button press event". That claim is false for this code, and the command is dead code that finishes the activity if ever exposed.

::diff{file="crates/tauri/mobile/android/src/main/java/app/tauri/AppPlugin.kt" lines="49-53"}

::callout{type="info"}
Either wire `exit` through the Rust command list, the permissions, and a TS helper, or remove it and correct the changelog.

The new entries in `build.rs` mark `register_listener` and `remove_listener` as default-allowed (`true`). The reference doc then adds them to the `default` permission set. Every app's default capability therefore allows these commands, but on desktop and iOS no Rust handler exists for them. Default-allowing commands that are not implemented in most targets widens the surface without any benefit there. It also makes the `allow-register-listener` permission misleading on platforms where it does nothing.

::diff{file="crates/tauri/build.rs" lines="164-169"}

::callout{type="info"}
Permissions for mobile-only commands should be scoped to mobile targets, or the commands should be implemented on every platform.
